#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""[" -f "${BASH_SOURCE:-$0}" ] || { echo "a Python script: run it with python3" >&2; exit 126; }; exec python3 "${BASH_SOURCE:-$0}" "$@" || exit # sh/bash: hand off to python3 before reading the docstring
Is the stall watchdog installed for one session, and is it current?

    check <config path>          # the session to report on
    STALL_WATCHDOG_CONFIG=... check

Prints ONE line and exits:

    0  installed, current      -> proceed
    1  installed, but stale, or a leg this script could not measure -> do not proceed
    2  not installed           -> follow SKILL.md

⚠️ STATE 1 IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS EXISTS, AND IT IS TWO THINGS AT ONCE.
Absent is loud: the daemon exits with WATCHDOG-CONFIG, systemctl shows failed,
somebody notices. Stale is silent - everything runs and the behaviour is subtly
the old one. So is BLIND: a watchdog whose captures all fail looks identical to a
healthy one from `systemctl status`, and the whole point of this service is to be
the thing nobody is watching. Both report 1, and the printed LINE says which,
because the code alone cannot carry that difference.

⚠️ IT VERIFIES REAL ARTIFACTS, NEVER A MARKER FILE. It imports watchdog.py with
this config, so every required key is resolved by the same loader the daemon uses;
it finds the systemd unit BY the config path it names, so a unit pointing somewhere
else cannot be mistaken for this one; and it compares the state file's mtime against
the service start and reads the capture_error a failed poll records in it, which
together are the only cheap proof that captures are actually landing. A marker file
can lie; none of those can.

⚠️ THE SERVICE IS FOUND ON THE HOST'S SERVICE MANAGER (fleetlib.services: systemd,
or launchd on macOS). Under systemd it is the system unit whose Environment= names
this config. Under launchd it is the job whose plist's EnvironmentVariables name it:
a LaunchDaemon in /Library/LaunchDaemons (system domain), else a LaunchAgent in
~/Library/LaunchAgents (the user's gui domain). ⚠️ A LAUNCHAGENT WATCHDOG IS
REPORTED UNPROTECTED, EXIT 1, EVEN WHEN IT IS CAPTURING: the agent it watches can
bootout, disable or delete a job in its own domain, and the protection is the point
of running the watchdog as a separate root-owned service. Only the LaunchDaemon
form, which runs as the agent's user under a root-owned definition, reads OK.
The directories are fleetlib.services' unit_dir(), so $FLEET_LAUNCHD_SYSTEM_DIR,
the fleet's one seam for launchd's system directory, replaces /Library/LaunchDaemons
here too (the tests use it; HOME moves the user's). A restart remedy under launchd
is the bootout and the bootstrap of the plist, never `kickstart -k`, which restarts
from the definition launchd loaded.

⚠️ REPORTS THE HOST IT IS ON. .install-state.json is committed, so it travels to
machines that never ran the install. Without the host key every one of them would
read "installed and current" while nothing is installed at all.

⚠️ THIS SCRIPT IS PER SESSION, NOT PER MACHINE. One agent's watchdog being fine
says nothing about another's. Run it once per config.

⚠️ EVERY PATH LEG PERFORMS THE DAEMON'S OWN OPERATION AND NEVER ASKS WHETHER THE
PATH EXISTS. exists(), isdir(), getmtime() and os.access() are all stat(2)-class:
they answer from the inode and open nothing, so each of them is green on a state
directory the daemon dies in. Three of those were here, and each had a state that
passed it while the watchdog could not start:

  * `os.access(STATE_DIR, W_OK)` ANSWERS A QUESTION ABOUT THE DIRECTORY, AND THE
    DAEMON DOES NOT WRITE "A FILE" INTO A DIRECTORY. It writes exactly two names,
    `<prefix>.json.tmp` (renamed over `<prefix>.json`) and `<prefix>.lock`, so a
    directory that is genuinely, fully writable while one of those two paths is a
    DIRECTORY or a mode-000 file passes W_OK and crashes acquire_singleton_lock()
    or save_state(). Measured 2026-09-09: with `<prefix>.lock` a directory,
    os.access(state_dir, W_OK) -> True and the check reported the whole state-dir
    section clean. That is the defect; the permission bits were never the point.
    ⚠️ DO NOT OVERSTATE THE REST OF IT, because a false mechanism is worse than
    no argument: on this kernel access(2) DOES catch a read-only remount
    (measured: EROFS, access -> False) and DOES catch the immutable flag, and for
    uid 0 its W_OK -> True is CORRECT because root really can write. What it
    still cannot see is an LSM denial and a container device/mount policy - and
    that half is argued from the syscall's documented behaviour, not measured
    here. The measured half above is on its own sufficient reason to write.
  * `os.path.exists(state_file)` followed by `getmtime` - stat succeeds on a
    file this user cannot read and on a DIRECTORY of that name, so a state path
    the daemon's load_state() dies on (PermissionError and IsADirectoryError are
    both outside its `except (FileNotFoundError, JSONDecodeError)`) reported OK,
    with a fresh mtime making the freshness leg pass too.
  * `os.path.exists(config)` before importing watchdog.py - a readable-looking
    config that cannot be opened surfaced as "watchdog.py does not load", a true
    sentence about the wrong file that sends the reader to debug the daemon.

⚠️ NEGATIVE CONTROLS, AND THE VERDICT THEY GATE. Against a TEMP COPY of a config
(never a live one): make `<state.dir>/<prefix>.lock` a directory; `chmod 000` the
state file; `chmod 000` the config. Each must exit non-zero naming that path and
handing over a command. ⚠️ IF ANY OF THOSE CONTROLS PASSES, ISSUE NO VERDICT
ABOUT THAT SESSION - a leg that stays green under its own control measures
nothing, and an OK from it is worse than no reading at all, because this is the
service nobody is watching. Three outcomes and only two are a verdict: control
red (the leg works), control green (report INCONCLUSIVE, not OK), control never
reached because an earlier leg exited first (also INCONCLUSIVE - the printed line
names the leg that spoke, so read it before concluding anything).

⚠️ THE REMEDIATIONS NAME AN ADMIN AND A COMMAND, AND EACH ERRNO GETS ITS OWN.
State directories are 0700 and configs 0600 by install; repairing one is machine
configuration, not an agent's work, and a line that describes the broken state
without handing over the exact `chmod`/`rm -f`/`install` is a line the blocked
reader cannot act on. ⚠️ ONE COMMAND FOR A CLASS OF CAUSES IS THE SAME DEFECT AS
NO COMMAND AT ALL, and worse, because it is confident: `chown` returns EROFS on a
read-only filesystem and EPERM against an immutable directory, `mkdir` does
nothing for a path that is a regular file, and `df`/`mount` say nothing about a
symlink loop. So EROFS, EPERM, EACCES, ENOTDIR/ELOOP and ENOENT each have their
own branch below. Do not group them together.

⚠️ THIS CHECK WRITES. It is not pure stat(2): it creates and removes one probe
file inside `state.dir` to prove the directory is genuinely writable. That directory is a systemd `ReadWritePaths=` entry belonging
to a LIVE session's daemon, so anyone wiring this script into an automated runner
is wiring a writer, not a reader. `fleet check-all` runs it, on demand, once per
linked skill; any further automated invoker inherits the same write. The probe's name carries
this process's pid so two concurrent runs cannot race - see PROBE_STEM for the
measured failure that rule comes from.

Every exit path in here has been driven by hand against a temp copy of the skill,
each asserted on its wording as well as its code. Re-drive them after changing any
branch: a check nobody has seen go red is a check nobody knows the meaning of.
"""
import errno
import glob
import json
import os
import re
import socket
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import time

HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
SKILL = os.path.dirname(HERE)
# The fleet tree this file lives in. realpath, never abspath: through a personal skill
# link abspath is the link path, and four parents up from there is not the fleet tree.
FLEET_ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(
    os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))))))
UNIT_DIRS = ("/etc/systemd/system", "/usr/lib/systemd/system", "/lib/systemd/system")
DARWIN = sys.platform == "darwin"


def fleet_services():
    """fleetlib.services, from the fleet tree this file lives in."""
    sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(FLEET_ROOT, "lib"))
    from fleetlib import services  # noqa: E402
    return services


def launchd_dirs():
    """(scope, directory) where a launchd watchdog job can live, protected first."""
    s = fleet_services()
    return tuple((scope, str(s.unit_dir(scope))) for scope in ("system", "user"))


def find_job(config_path: str):
    """(label, scope, plist path) of the launchd job whose EnvironmentVariables name
    THIS config, or None: found by content, for the reason find_unit gives."""
    import plistlib
    want = os.path.realpath(config_path)
    for scope, d in launchd_dirs():
        for path in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(d, "*.plist"))):
            try:
                with open(path, "rb") as f:
                    job = plistlib.load(f)
            except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - any unreadable or malformed plist is not ours
                continue
            env = job.get("EnvironmentVariables") if isinstance(job, dict) else None
            named = env.get("STALL_WATCHDOG_CONFIG") if isinstance(env, dict) else None
            if isinstance(named, str) and os.path.realpath(named) == want:
                return str(job.get("Label") or os.path.basename(path)[:-len(".plist")]), scope, path
    return None


def restart_hint() -> str:
    """The command an admin runs to restart the watchdog, before its unit is known."""
    try:
        s = fleet_services()
        launchd, system_dir = s.backend() == "launchd", s.unit_dir("system")
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - no library: the platform's word, and the default directory
        launchd, system_dir = DARWIN, "/Library/LaunchDaemons"
    if launchd:
        return ("`sudo launchctl bootout system/<that label>`, then `sudo launchctl bootstrap system "
                f"{system_dir}/<that label>.plist`")
    return "`sudo systemctl restart <that unit>`"


def state_path() -> str:
    """The install record, `installation/.install-state.json` in this skill's install
    context. It is SPECIFIC - it describes one install - so it lives with the install,
    never beside this script, and fleetlib is the one resolver for where that is."""
    sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(FLEET_ROOT, "lib"))
    from fleetlib import paths  # noqa: E402
    return str(paths.skill_context("stall-watchdog") / "installation" / ".install-state.json")


def out(code: int, msg: str) -> None:
    print(msg)
    sys.exit(code)


def declared_version() -> int:
    """The version SKILL.md declares. Absent means the skill predates the
    convention, which is itself a reason to re-install rather than to guess."""
    with open(os.path.join(SKILL, "SKILL.md"), encoding="utf-8") as f:
        head = f.read(4096)
    m = re.search(r"^version:\s*(\d+)\s*$", head, re.M)
    return int(m.group(1)) if m else 0


def find_unit(config_path: str):
    """The unit that names THIS config, or None.

    Found by content rather than by filename: the unit is named per session and this
    script is universal, so a name pattern here would be one install's convention
    hardcoded into a common file. Matching on the config path also means a unit that
    watches a DIFFERENT session can never be reported as this one's.
    """
    want = os.path.realpath(config_path)
    for d in UNIT_DIRS:
        for path in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(d, "*.service"))):
            try:
                with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
                    text = f.read()
            except OSError:
                continue
            for line in text.splitlines():
                line = line.strip()
                if not line.startswith("Environment="):
                    continue
                m = re.search(r"STALL_WATCHDOG_CONFIG=(\S+)", line)
                if m and os.path.realpath(m.group(1).strip('"')) == want:
                    return os.path.basename(path)
    return None


def whoami():
    """This process's real user NAME, resolved rather than read from $USER.

    ⚠️ NEVER PUT A LITERAL `$USER` IN A REMEDIATION. A message that has already
    had to fall back because $USER is unset must not then hand the operator
    `sudo chown $USER <path>`: under systemd-run, `su` without `-` and
    `docker exec` that variable is unset, and the command fails with `the
    following required arguments were not provided: <FILE>` - the one line the
    already-blocked reader copy-pastes does not run. This service is INSTALLED
    UNDER SYSTEMD, so the scrubbed-environment case is the normal one here, not
    an exotic one."""
    try:
        import pwd as _pwd
        return _pwd.getpwuid(os.getuid()).pw_name
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - a uid with no passwd entry is routine in a container
        return os.environ.get("USER") or os.environ.get("LOGNAME") or f"uid {os.getuid()}"


def errname(e):
    """A legible name for an OSError's errno.

    ⚠️ OSError.errno CAN BE None - TimeoutError, InterruptedError and the whole
    ConnectionError family are OSError subclasses that may carry none - and
    formatting one straight into errno.errorcode.get renders `(None: None)` in
    the exact sentence the operator is going to act on."""
    if e.errno is None:
        return type(e).__name__
    return errno.errorcode.get(e.errno, str(e.errno))


def file_kind(mode):
    for pred, name in ((stat.S_ISDIR, "a directory"), (stat.S_ISFIFO, "a FIFO"),
                       (stat.S_ISSOCK, "a socket"), (stat.S_ISCHR, "a character device"),
                       (stat.S_ISBLK, "a block device")):
        if pred(mode):
            return name
    return "not a regular file"


def wrong_shape(err):
    """"This path is not a usable regular file", as against a permission or a
    resource problem. They need different remediations, and one line for both is
    the failure this whole file is about."""
    return err.errno in (errno.EISDIR, errno.ENOTDIR, errno.ELOOP,
                         errno.ENAMETOOLONG, errno.EINVAL)


def read_text_head(path, cap=1 << 20):
    """Open PATH the way load_state() does and decode it -> (ok, OSError-or-None).

    THE REAL OPERATION. See the header: stat(2) answers from the inode, and every
    one of these paths is consumed by opening it, so opening it is the only test
    whose green means anything.

    ⚠️ TEXT, NOT BYTES, AND THAT IS THE POINT. watchdog.py's load_state() is
    `json.load(open(path))` under `except (FileNotFoundError, JSONDecodeError)`,
    so a state file containing invalid UTF-8 raises UnicodeDecodeError, which it
    does NOT catch, and the daemon dies at startup. A one-byte binary read is
    green on exactly that file. Decoding is what closes the hole.

    ⚠️ CAPPED, because an unbounded read of a path this process does not control
    is a memory bomb dressed as thoroughness. A capped read cannot prove the
    WHOLE file decodes, and that residual is real: a valid prefix followed by a
    bad byte beyond the cap still passes here and still kills the daemon. The
    cap is 1 MiB against a state file measured in tens of bytes, so the gap is
    unreachable in practice and stated rather than hidden.

    ⚠️ O_NONBLOCK AND THE S_ISREG TEST STOP THIS FROM HANGING. A plain open() on
    a FIFO blocks forever waiting for a writer, and the state directory is a path
    this check does not own. A readiness check that never returns is worse than
    one that is wrong - it is indistinguishable from a slow machine, nothing
    times it out, and a session that hangs with nobody at the terminal stays
    hung for hours."""
    try:
        fd = os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY | getattr(os, "O_NONBLOCK", 0))
    except OSError as e:
        return False, e
    try:
        st = os.fstat(fd)
        if not stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode):
            err = OSError(errno.EINVAL, f"is {file_kind(st.st_mode)}, not a regular file")
            err.filename = path
            return False, err
        raw = os.read(fd, cap)
    except OSError as e:
        return False, e
    finally:
        os.close(fd)
    try:
        raw.decode("utf-8")
    except UnicodeDecodeError as e:
        err = OSError(errno.EILSEQ, f"is not valid UTF-8 ({e.reason} at byte {e.start}), so "
                                    "load_state()'s json.load raises UnicodeDecodeError, "
                                    "which it does not catch")
        err.filename = path
        return False, err
    return True, None


# ⚠️ THE PROBE FILE'S NAME CARRIES THIS PROCESS'S PID, AND A CONSTANT NAME IS A
# BUG. Two runs of this check against the SAME config race on a shared name: A's
# os.replace moves the shared tmp away and B's os.replace then gets ENOENT on a
# perfectly writable directory, which the caller reports as "state dir does not
# exist. install -d ...". The operator runs a command that succeeds and changes
# nothing, re-runs, and it passes - which teaches them the check is flaky, and a
# flaky gate trains its readers to excuse red. Measured: 12 concurrent probers,
# 5-7 spurious ENOENTs per round, 6 rounds out of 6.
PROBE_STEM = f".stall-watchdog-check-probe.{os.getpid()}"


def probe_writable_dir(directory, stem):
    """Perform save_state()'s OWN sequence in DIRECTORY -> OSError or None.

    create <stem>.tmp, write, os.replace it over <stem>, unlink. That is
    watchdog.py's save_state shape, not a generic touch: the rename is a separate
    kernel operation with its own failure modes (a cross-device state dir, an
    immutable target) and skipping it would leave the one syscall that actually
    lands the state file unmeasured.

    ⚠️ NO fsync, DELIBERATELY, AND ADDING ONE MAKES THIS CHECK STRICTER THAN THE
    DAEMON IT REPORTS ON. save_state() is `open(tmp,"w")`, `json.dump`,
    `os.replace` - there is no fsync in it and no directory fsync either. On a
    mount where fsync fails but write+rename succeed (9p, virtiofs and several
    FUSE and container overlays return EINVAL or ENOSYS; some network mounts
    return EIO) an fsync here would return that errno and this check would report
    NOT INSTALLED on a machine where the watchdog runs perfectly. A check may be
    weaker than its subject; it may not be stronger. If durability is ever wanted
    here, it belongs in save_state first and in this probe second.

    ⚠️ THE unlink RUNS EVEN WHEN THE WRITE FAILED, and the cleanup's own errors
    are swallowed on purpose: a readiness check that leaves debris in a directory
    systemd has declared ReadWritePaths= turns a diagnostic into a change to the
    machine. The probe names itself for this script AND this pid, so a leftover -
    if the process is killed between the write and the unlink - is attributable
    rather than mysterious, and so two concurrent runs cannot collide."""
    tmp = os.path.join(directory, stem + ".tmp")
    dst = os.path.join(directory, stem)
    try:
        fd = os.open(tmp, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, 0o600)
        try:
            os.write(fd, b"probe\n")
        finally:
            os.close(fd)
        os.replace(tmp, dst)
    except OSError as e:
        return e
    finally:
        for p in (tmp, dst):
            try:
                os.unlink(p)
            except OSError:
                pass
    return None


def probe_openable(path, mode):
    """Open PATH the way the daemon does -> OSError or None; a plain absent path
    is None.

    MODE is os.O_RDONLY or os.O_WRONLY. NO O_CREAT and NO O_TRUNC, deliberately:
    this runs against a LIVE session's state directory, the lock file is held
    open by a running daemon, and a check that truncates or creates the very
    files it is reporting on has changed the thing it measured. (The daemon's own
    open is `open(path,"w")`, i.e. O_TRUNC - this probe is strictly gentler.)

    ⚠️ O_NONBLOCK, so a FIFO at this path cannot hang the check. O_WRONLY on a
    FIFO with no reader is the textbook indefinite block; with O_NONBLOCK it
    returns ENXIO. The fstat then reports a non-regular file as its own finding.

    ⚠️ ENOENT IS NOT AUTOMATICALLY "NO FINDING", AND THE DANGLING SYMLINK IS WHY.
    Absent means the daemon has not written that path yet, which the freshness
    legs judge with far better information than this function has. But a DANGLING
    SYMLINK also opens ENOENT while genuinely EXISTING, and the daemon dies on it
    exactly as it dies on a mode-000 file - so lexists() separates the two.
    Collapsing them puts a real, silent breakage back under a carve-out written
    for a legitimate state."""
    try:
        fd = os.open(path, mode | getattr(os, "O_NONBLOCK", 0))
    except OSError as e:
        if e.errno == errno.ENOENT and not os.path.lexists(path):
            return None
        return e
    try:
        st = os.fstat(fd)
        if not stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode):
            err = OSError(errno.EINVAL, f"is {file_kind(st.st_mode)}, not a regular file")
            err.filename = path
            return err
    except OSError as e:
        return e
    finally:
        os.close(fd)
    return None


def systemctl(*args):
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(("systemctl",) + args, capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=20)
    except (FileNotFoundError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as e:
        return None, f"{type(e).__name__}: {e}"
    return r.stdout.strip(), None


def main() -> None:
    host = socket.gethostname()

    config = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else os.environ.get("STALL_WATCHDOG_CONFIG")
    if not config:
        out(2, "NOT INSTALLED: no config selected. Pass the config path as an argument, "
               "or set STALL_WATCHDOG_CONFIG. This service is installed per SESSION, so "
               "there is nothing to check until you say which one.")
    config = os.path.expanduser(config)
    # OPENED, not stat'ed: watchdog.py's module scope reads this file, so an
    # unreadable one would otherwise surface below as "watchdog.py does not load".
    # The byte count is deliberately DISCARDED here: this leg's requirement is
    # "readable", and an empty or malformed config is watchdog.py's own loader to
    # reject, in its own WATCHDOG-CONFIG wording naming the missing key. Adding
    # an emptiness rule here would make this check stricter than the install it
    # reports on, and would pre-empt a better diagnostic with a worse one.
    ok, err = read_text_head(config)
    if not ok:
        if err.errno == errno.ENOENT:
            out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: no config at {config}. Run: `install -m 0600 "
                   f"{os.path.join(SKILL, 'config.example.json')} {config}`, then fill it "
                   f"in per SKILL.md step 2.")
        if wrong_shape(err):
            # ⚠️ "SOME COMPONENT OF", not "this file". ENOTDIR and ELOOP are
            # raised just as readily by a PARENT component, and a remediation
            # that assumes the final one hands over `rm -rf <full path> &&
            # install ...` whose second half fails with "No such file or
            # directory" - measured. Naming the path and letting the operator
            # find the offending component is less satisfying and correct.
            out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: some component of {config} is not a readable regular "
                   f"file ({errname(err)}: {err.strerror or err}). `ls -ld` each component "
                   f"to find it, remove what is in the way, and put the config there: "
                   f"`install -m 0600 {os.path.join(SKILL, 'config.example.json')} "
                   f"{config}`, then fill it in per SKILL.md step 2.")
        out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: the config at {config} exists but cannot be read by "
               f"{whoami()} ({errname(err)}: {err.strerror or err}), so the daemon cannot "
               f"start either - it reads this file at import. A machine ADMIN repairs it: "
               f"`sudo chown {whoami()} {config} && chmod 0600 {config}`.")

    # Import the daemon itself, with this config. Its module scope loads the file and
    # resolves every required key; a SystemExit here is its own WATCHDOG-CONFIG
    # diagnostic, which is better worded than anything this script could invent.
    sys.path.insert(0, SKILL)
    os.environ["STALL_WATCHDOG_CONFIG"] = config
    try:
        import watchdog  # noqa: E402
    except SystemExit as e:
        out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: {e}")
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 - any import failure is "not installed"
        out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: watchdog.py does not load ({type(e).__name__}: {e})")

    # THE DIRECTORY, WRITTEN. probe_writable_dir replays save_state()'s own
    # create/write/fsync/rename/unlink rather than asking access(2) whether the
    # bits permit it - see the header for the four states that pass W_OK and
    # crash the daemon. The errno branches below are separate because their fixes
    # are: a missing directory is created, an unwritable one is chown'd, and
    # neither command does anything for a path that is a regular file.
    state_dir = watchdog.STATE_DIR
    err = probe_writable_dir(state_dir, PROBE_STEM)
    if err is not None:
        who = whoami()
        # ⚠️ ONE REMEDIATION PER CAUSE, AND THE BRANCHES BELOW ARE NOT
        # INTERCHANGEABLE. Every one of these errnos reaches this point, and the
        # command that fixes one is a command that FAILS for the next: chown
        # returns EROFS on a read-only filesystem and EPERM on an immutable
        # directory, `mkdir` does nothing for a path that is a regular file, and
        # `df`/`mount` say nothing about a symlink loop. Grouping them is the
        # exact "one confident prescription for causes it cannot distinguish"
        # defect this file exists to remove; do not re-flatten them.
        if err.errno == errno.ENOENT:
            why = ("the watchdog keeps its lock and cursor in it; launchd creates a missing log "
                   "directory itself, but with mode 0744 (measured in the user domain) and, for "
                   "this root-owned LaunchDaemon, presumably owned by root (not measured)"
                   if DARWIN else "it is a ReadWritePaths= entry, and systemd fails the unit at "
                   "start (226/NAMESPACE) if it is missing")
            out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: state dir {state_dir} does not exist. Create it "
                   f"before enabling the service - {why}: "
                   f"`install -d -m 0700 {state_dir}`.")
        if err.errno in (errno.ENOTDIR, errno.ELOOP, errno.ENAMETOOLONG):
            out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: state.dir names {state_dir}, and some component of that "
                   f"path is not a usable directory ({errname(err)}: "
                   f"{err.strerror or err}); the lock and the cursor cannot live there. "
                   f"`ls -ld` each component to find the one in the way, remove it, then "
                   f"`install -d -m 0700 {state_dir}`.")
        if err.errno == errno.EROFS:
            out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: state dir {state_dir} is on a READ-ONLY filesystem "
                   f"({err.strerror or err}), so the daemon can never write its cursor or "
                   f"take its lock. chown and chmod BOTH return EROFS here and neither is "
                   f"the fix. A machine ADMIN remounts it read-write, or moves state.dir: "
                   f"`df -P {state_dir}` names its mount point (the last column), and "
                   f"`mount` shows how that is mounted.")
        if err.errno == errno.EPERM:
            # EPERM rather than EACCES on a directory this user can otherwise
            # write is the immutable-attribute signature, which is precisely the
            # state access(2) DOES catch and chown does NOT fix.
            if DARWIN:
                flag = (f"`ls -lOd {state_dir}` and clears it with `sudo chflags nouchg,noschg "
                        f"{state_dir}`; if ls shows neither flag, treat it as a sandbox or TCC "
                        f"denial (a protected folder) and read the system log")
            else:
                flag = (f"`lsattr -d {state_dir}` and clears it with `sudo chattr -i "
                        f"{state_dir}`; if lsattr shows no `i`, treat it as an LSM denial and "
                        f"read the audit log")
            out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: state dir {state_dir} refused a write with EPERM "
                   f"({err.strerror or err}). On a directory whose mode already permits "
                   f"the write this is the immutable attribute, not ownership - `chown` "
                   f"itself returns EPERM against it. A machine ADMIN reads {flag}.")
        if err.errno == errno.EACCES:
            out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: state dir {state_dir} exists but {who} cannot write "
                   f"into it ({err.strerror or err}); the lock and the cursor live there, "
                   f"so the daemon dies at start. A machine ADMIN repairs it: "
                   f"`sudo chown -R {who} {state_dir} && chmod 0700 {state_dir}`.")
        out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: state dir {state_dir} exists but a write into it failed "
               f"({errname(err)}: {err.strerror or err}). This is neither a permission "
               f"problem nor a wrong path shape - both are handled above - so chown, chmod "
               f"and mkdir will all fail or do nothing. ENOSPC and a full inode table land "
               f"here: a machine ADMIN reads `df -h {state_dir}` and "
               f"`df -i {state_dir}`.")

    # THE TWO PATHS THE DAEMON ACTUALLY OPENS, opened the way it opens them. The
    # directory being writable says nothing about these: watchdog.py does not
    # write "a file" here, it writes exactly these two names, and either of them
    # existing as a directory or as a mode-000 file crashes the daemon on a state
    # dir that passes every test above. ENOENT is not a finding (see
    # probe_openable) - the freshness legs below judge absence with far better
    # information than this leg has.
    lock_file = os.path.join(state_dir, watchdog.STATE_PREFIX + ".lock")
    err = probe_openable(lock_file, os.O_WRONLY)
    if err is not None:
        # ⚠️ THE REMEDIATION DOES NOT ENUMERATE SHAPES IT CANNOT SEE. An earlier
        # draft offered "chown if it is a file, rm -rf if it is a directory",
        # which covers neither a symlink loop nor a dangling symlink nor a FIFO -
        # all of which reach here. `rm -f` removes every one of them, and the
        # daemon recreates the lock on its next start, so ONE command is correct
        # for the whole class and no enumeration is needed.
        out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: the singleton lock {lock_file} is present but cannot be "
               f"opened for writing ({errname(err)}: {err.strerror or err}), so "
               f"acquire_singleton_lock() raises before the daemon polls anything - on a "
               f"state dir that IS writable. The lock carries no state: a machine ADMIN "
               f"removes it and restarts the unit that names "
               f"STALL_WATCHDOG_CONFIG={config} - `sudo rm -f {lock_file}` (add `-r` if it "
               f"is a directory), then {restart_hint()}.")

    try:
        services = fleet_services()
        backend = services.backend()
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 - fleetlib's refusal is the whole finding
        out(1, f"NO VERDICT: the service manager cannot be asked ({type(e).__name__}: {e}), "
               f"so whether the watchdog runs and captures is UNMEASURED here.")
    unprotected = None
    if backend == "launchd":
        job = find_job(config)
        if job is None:
            out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: no launchd job names STALL_WATCHDOG_CONFIG={config} "
                   f"({' or '.join(d for _, d in launchd_dirs())}). Install one from "
                   f"installation/agent-stall-watchdog.plist.example, per SKILL.md steps 4 to 6.")
        unit, scope, plist = job
        domain = services.domain(scope)
        st = services.status(unit, scope)
        if st["load"] == "unknown":
            out(1, f"NO VERDICT: {plist} names this config, but launchctl could not report "
                   f"{domain}/{unit} ({st['error']}), so whether the service runs and captures "
                   f"is UNMEASURED here. Confirm by hand before relying on it.")
        if st["load"] == "not-found":
            out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: {plist} names this config but launchd has not loaded it, "
                   f"so it does not run: {'sudo ' if scope == 'system' else ''}launchctl "
                   f"bootstrap {domain} {plist}")
        if st["active"] != "active":
            out(1, f"STALE: {unit} is loaded in {domain} but '{st['active']}' (last result "
                   f"{st['result']}). The session it watches is unsupervised right now. "
                   f"{services.hint('log', unit, scope)}")
        if scope == "user":
            unprotected = (f"UNPROTECTED: {unit} runs as a LaunchAgent in {domain}, so the agent "
                           f"it watches can bootout, disable or delete it. Install the "
                           f"LaunchDaemon form instead (SKILL.md step 6): render "
                           f"installation/agent-stall-watchdog.plist.example, `sudo install -m 644 "
                           f"-o root -g wheel <rendered> {services.unit_dir('system')}/{unit}.plist && "
                           f"sudo launchctl bootstrap system {services.unit_dir('system')}/{unit}.plist`, "
                           f"then `launchctl bootout {domain}/{unit}` and remove {plist}.")
    else:
        scope = "system"
        unit = find_unit(config)
        if unit is None:
            out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: no systemd unit names STALL_WATCHDOG_CONFIG={config}. "
                   f"Install one from installation/agent-stall-watchdog.service.example, "
                   f"per SKILL.md steps 4 to 6.")

        enabled, err = systemctl("is-enabled", unit)
        if err:
            out(1, f"NO VERDICT: {unit} exists but systemctl could not be run ({err}), so "
                   f"whether the service is enabled and capturing is UNMEASURED here. "
                   f"Confirm by hand before relying on it.")
        if enabled not in ("enabled", "enabled-runtime", "static"):
            out(2, f"NOT INSTALLED: {unit} is present but '{enabled}'. It will not start on "
                   f"boot: sudo systemctl enable --now {unit}")

        active, _ = systemctl("is-active", unit)
        if active != "active":
            out(1, f"STALE: {unit} is installed and enabled but '{active}'. The session it "
                   f"watches is unsupervised right now. journalctl -u {unit} -n 30")

    # Proof of CAPTURE, not merely of running. Every poll saves the state file, and
    # a poll whose capture failed saves it with `capture_error` (watchdog.py's
    # CaptureError), so the proof is a state file newer than the service start that
    # holds no capture_error. A file older than the start is a watchdog that has not
    # completed one poll; a capture_error is one that polls and cannot see. Both are
    # up and blind, which the service manager reports as healthy. The start is the
    # running process's (services.started_at); an active service whose start cannot
    # be read is NO VERDICT, never a skipped leg.
    state_file = os.path.join(state_dir, watchdog.STATE_PREFIX + ".json")
    started = services.started_at(unit, scope)
    if started is None:
        out(1, f"NO VERDICT: {unit} is active, but when its process started cannot be read, "
               f"so whether its captures are landing is UNMEASURED here. Confirm by hand: "
               f"{services.hint('status', unit, scope)}")
    ran_for = time.time() - started
    # OPENED, not exists()-ed. load_state() catches FileNotFoundError and
    # JSONDecodeError and NOTHING ELSE, so a state file this user cannot read
    # - or a DIRECTORY of that name - kills the daemon at startup while
    # exists() says yes and getmtime() below happily returns a fresh
    # timestamp, which greened the whole freshness section. Absence is still
    # handled by the two branches after this one: it is a legitimate state,
    # unreadability never is.
    st_ok, st_err = read_text_head(state_file)
    # ⚠️ A DANGLING SYMLINK OPENS ENOENT WHILE GENUINELY EXISTING, and the
    # daemon dies on it exactly as it dies on a mode-000 file. lexists()
    # separates it from the legitimate absent case below, which the freshness
    # branches judge with far better information than an open() has.
    if not st_ok and (st_err.errno != errno.ENOENT or os.path.lexists(state_file)):
        out(1, f"STALE: {state_file} is present but cannot be read as the daemon reads "
               f"it ({errname(st_err)}: {st_err.strerror or st_err}), so watchdog.py's "
               f"load_state() raises before the first poll - it catches "
               f"FileNotFoundError and JSONDecodeError and nothing else. This session "
               f"is UNSUPERVISED. The state file is a rebuildable cursor, not a record: "
               f"a machine ADMIN removes it and restarts the unit that names "
               f"STALL_WATCHDOG_CONFIG={config} - `sudo rm -f {state_file}` (add `-r` "
               f"if it is a directory), then {restart_hint()}.")
    if not st_ok:  # a genuinely absent file - every other case exited above.
        if ran_for < watchdog.POLL_SECS * 2:
            out(1, f"NO VERDICT: {unit} started {ran_for:.0f}s ago and has not written "
                   f"{state_file} yet; the first poll is due within "
                   f"{watchdog.POLL_SECS:.0f}s. Too early to tell - re-run.")
        # ⚠️ NOT A CAPTURE FAILURE, AND THIS LINE MUST NOT GUESS ONE. A failed
        # capture is recorded in the state file (the capture_error branch below
        # names its cause), so a watchdog that never wrote it has not finished a
        # single poll: it exits at start, or cannot save. Its log says which.
        out(1, f"STALE: {unit} has run {ran_for:.0f}s and never wrote {state_file}: it has "
               f"not completed one poll, so it is up and BLIND. A failed capture would have "
               f"been recorded there, so read its log for the reason: "
               f"{services.hint('log', unit, scope)}")
    age = os.path.getmtime(state_file)
    if time.time() - age > ran_for:
        out(1, f"STALE: {state_file} predates this run of {unit} (started "
               f"{ran_for:.0f}s ago). Nothing has been captured since it started: "
               f"up and BLIND. See technical/watchdog.md section 11.")
    # THE CAUSE, IN THE DAEMON'S OWN WORDS. A state file written by a poll whose
    # capture failed is fresh, so the legs above pass it: only its capture_error
    # says the watchdog is blind. The TMUX_TMPDIR hint belongs to a tmux that RAN
    # and failed; a tmux that cannot be started is the job's PATH, and pointing
    # that reader at TMUX_TMPDIR sends them to the wrong file.
    try:
        with open(state_file, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            saved = json.load(f)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        saved = {}
    cap = saved.get("capture_error") if isinstance(saved, dict) else None
    if isinstance(cap, dict):
        when = cap.get("at")
        ago = f", {time.time() - when:.0f}s ago" if isinstance(when, (int, float)) else ""
        if cap.get("kind") == "start":
            if backend == "launchd":
                fix = ("Re-render the job with installation/render-plist.py (`/adding-an-agent` "
                       "section 5 step 7 runs it), which puts the directory of the tmux the "
                       "session runs on the job's PATH, and install it again with the root "
                       "command that step prints")
            else:
                fix = ("The unit runs with systemd's default PATH: install tmux as /usr/bin/tmux, "
                       "where the session unit runs it from")
            out(1, f"STALE: {unit} is up and BLIND: it cannot start tmux{ago} "
                   f"({cap.get('error')}). {fix}.")
        where = ("the job's TMUX_TMPDIR" if backend == "launchd"
                 else "the unit's Environment=TMUX_TMPDIR=")
        out(1, f"STALE: {unit} is up and BLIND: its last capture failed{ago} "
               f"({cap.get('error')}). Check {where}, and tmux.tmpdir against the live "
               f"session.")

    # Captures are landing. A watchdog the agent can switch off is still not the
    # service this skill installs: said before the version, which cannot change it.
    if unprotected:
        out(1, unprotected)

    # Artifacts are present and captures are landing. Only the recorded version can
    # still be wrong.
    want = declared_version()
    # The record lives in the install context. A context that does not resolve is a
    # leg this script cannot measure, which is 1 with the reason in words - never a
    # guess at a path, and never "not recorded", which would send the operator to
    # re-run an install step that cannot fix it.
    try:
        record_path = state_path()
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 - fleetlib's refusal is the whole finding
        out(1, f"NO VERDICT on the recorded version: the install context does not "
               f"resolve ({type(e).__name__}: {e}). Run this with FLEET_CONTEXT_ROOT "
               f"set to the install's checkout, or from inside it.")
    # ONE operation. exists()-then-open reported an unreadable state file under
    # "no .install-state.json records which version" - a claim about a file that
    # is sitting right there, and the operator then re-runs an install step that
    # cannot fix a mode.
    try:
        with open(record_path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            recorded = json.load(f)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        out(1, f"STALE: installed, but no .install-state.json at {record_path} records which "
               f"version (SKILL.md declares {want}). Re-run SKILL.md step 7.")
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
        out(1, f"STALE: .install-state.json at {record_path} unreadable ({type(e).__name__}: {e}). "
               f"Run `chmod 0644 {record_path}`, or delete it and re-run SKILL.md step 7 to "
               f"rewrite it.")

    entry = (recorded.get("hosts", {}).get(host, {}) or {}).get(config)
    if entry is None:
        out(1, f"STALE: nothing recorded for {config} on {host}. The state file is "
               f"committed and travels to machines that never ran the install, so an "
               f"unrecorded host is an unverified one. Re-run SKILL.md step 7.")
    have = int(entry.get("version", -1))
    if have < want:
        out(1, f"STALE: installed version {have}, SKILL.md declares {want}. "
               f"Re-run SKILL.md.")
    if have > want:
        out(1, f"STALE: installed version {have} is NEWER than SKILL.md's {want}. "
               f"The fleet tree this check runs from ({FLEET_ROOT}) is older than the "
               f"install that was recorded: `fleet upgrade` to the tag that made it.")

    out(0, f"OK: installed and current (version {want}) on {host} - {unit} active, "
           f"watching {watchdog.TMUX_TARGET} in {watchdog.TMUX_TMPDIR}, "
           f"stall {watchdog.STALL_SECS:.0f}s, state {state_file}")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
